= Polysemy — Simple =

'''Polysemy''' is when one word has several related meanings.

For example, “mouth” can mean a person's mouth, the opening of a cave, or the mouth of a river.

Those meanings are different, but they are connected.

Polysemy is different from an unrelated coincidence where the same word form has two separate meanings. It is also only one part of [[Ambiguity/Simple|ambiguity]].

[[Context (linguistics)/Simple|Context]] usually helps people understand which sense is intended.

Linguists study polysemy because it shows that word meaning is often flexible rather than one word = one fixed definition.

